{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792695157,"nonce":"2rGTwZCKlg+HGmUqzM8RgA7AAEs=","title":"Compiler: prove per-field ownership before drop glue frees reference fields","message":"drop_glue.rs treats every named reference field as owned, so it runs only with ZYNTAX_DROP_GLUE=1 and skips Optional\u003cNamed\u003e tagged-union fields. Replace the assumption with a proof, so glue is safe to run by default for every frontend and the env var goes away.\n\nApproach (from a survey of Rayzor's instance freeing, unverified by running it): a whole-module census over every store to a (type, field); a field is owned only when every store is a fresh single-holder allocation (a new instance or a call that returns fresh storage, with no other holder). Any unproven store leaves the field borrowed, so aliasing leaks and never double-frees. Add the matching confinement check (a value loaded out of the owner may alias the child) and a guard against releasing twice. Treat a null pointer as absent. For Optional\u003cNamed\u003e, test the tag and release the payload. For Array\u003cT\u003e, loop over elements only when the array is proven owned and its elements are fresh. No runtime ownership mask.\n\nCross-module behaviour of the census is unverified. Shared nodes and parent pointers will leak; only a refcount would address that and it is out of scope.\n\nBlocks: TypeScript class slice 1b (3497149), owning reference fields and C | null. Slice 1 (scalar-field classes) does not need it.","files":null}]}